var e=[{topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A conventional tablet releases all its drug at once, so the plasma level spikes and then falls. Modified release dosage forms change either when the drug comes out or how fast it comes out, so that the plasma level stays inside the therapeutic window for longer.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The terms, kept apart`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Term`,`What it means`,`Example`],rows:[[`Immediate release`,`All the drug is released at once`,`Ordinary paracetamol tablet`],[`Delayed release`,`Release starts at a time or site other than immediately`,`Enteric coated aspirin`],[`Sustained release`,`Initial dose plus slow maintenance release; rate need not be constant`,`Wax matrix tablet`],[`Controlled release`,`Release at a predetermined, usually constant, rate`,`Osmotic pump tablet`],[`Extended release`,`Allows at least a twofold cut in dosing frequency`,`Umbrella for SR and CR`],[`Repeat action`,`A second full dose is released after an interval`,`Press-coated tablet`],[`Targeted release`,`Release directed to a particular site or tissue`,`Colon-targeted mesalazine`]],caption:`Modified release is the parent term. It covers delayed release and extended release; extended release covers sustained and controlled release.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Sustained is not the same as controlled`,text:`Sustained release only means the effect lasts longer, and the release usually follows first-order kinetics that slows as the tablet empties. Controlled release means the rate is predetermined and generally zero-order, so the plasma concentration is held steady. Every controlled release form is sustained, but not every sustained release form is controlled.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Why bother`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Fewer doses per day, so patients actually take the medicine.`,`Smaller peak to trough swing, so fewer concentration-related side effects.`,`Night-time cover without waking the patient.`,`Often a smaller total amount of drug over 24 hours.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Dose dumping: if the controlling mechanism fails, the whole dose is released at once and toxicity follows.`,`No dose flexibility, and most of these tablets must not be crushed or chewed.`,`Poorer in vitro to in vivo correlation, so bioequivalence is harder to show.`,`Higher cost, and a longer time for the drug to be cleared if an adverse reaction occurs.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`A sustained release product usually has two parts: a loading (initial) dose that acts at once to reach the therapeutic level, and a maintenance dose that is released slowly to replace what the body eliminates.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The one-line definition to reproduce`,text:`Controlled release delivers drug at a predetermined rate, locally or systemically, for a specified period of time, so that the plasma concentration is maintained rather than allowed to fluctuate.`}]},{topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Two things carry most of the marks here: which drug is a suitable candidate, and which mechanism gives which release order and which mathematical model.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Selecting the drug candidate`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Property`,`Ideal for controlled release`,`Unsuitable`],rows:[[`Biological half-life`,`2 to 8 hours`,`Below 2 h (dose too large) or above 8 h (already long acting)`],[`Single dose`,`Not more than about 0.5 to 1.0 g`,`Very large doses, the tablet becomes unswallowable`],[`Therapeutic index`,`Wide`,`Narrow, e.g. digoxin, lithium, because of dose dumping`],[`Absorption`,`Absorbed uniformly along the gut`,`Narrow absorption window in the upper gut`],[`Aqueous solubility`,`Moderate, above about 0.1 mg per mL`,`Very poorly soluble; dissolution already limits it`],[`Partition coefficient`,`Log P roughly 1 to 3`,`Very hydrophilic or very lipophilic`],[`Molecular weight`,`Below about 1000 Da`,`Large molecules diffuse too slowly`],[`GI stability`,`Stable across the gut`,`Acid labile or degraded in colon`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Absorption window drugs`,text:`Riboflavin, levodopa, ferrous salts, acyclovir, captopril and furosemide are absorbed mainly from the duodenum and upper jejunum. A conventional controlled release tablet simply carries the drug past the window and out, so these drugs need a gastroretentive system instead.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Release mechanisms`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Mechanism`,`Design`,`Release order`],rows:[[`Diffusion, reservoir`,`Core surrounded by a rate-controlling polymer membrane`,`Zero order while the core stays saturated`],[`Diffusion, matrix`,`Drug dispersed through an insoluble polymer`,`Square root of time (Higuchi), not zero order`],[`Dissolution, encapsulation`,`Coats of different thickness on drug pellets`,`Pulsed or apparently first order`],[`Dissolution, matrix`,`Drug embedded in a slowly dissolving wax or polymer`,`First order or erosion controlled`],[`Osmotic pump`,`Semipermeable membrane with a laser-drilled orifice`,`Zero order, independent of pH and stirring`],[`Ion exchange resin`,`Drug bound to a resin as a resinate`,`Depends on gut ionic strength`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Reservoir systems give the cleanest zero-order profile but will dose-dump if the membrane cracks, so the dose is often divided among many coated pellets.`,`Matrix systems are cheap, robust and easy to make; hydroxypropyl methylcellulose is the standard hydrophilic matrix polymer and ethylcellulose the standard insoluble one.`,`The elementary osmotic pump has a core of drug plus an osmogen such as sodium chloride or mannitol inside a cellulose acetate membrane. Water is drawn in osmotically and pushes a saturated solution out of the orifice at a constant rate.`,`For poorly soluble drugs the push-pull osmotic pump adds a swelling polyethylene oxide layer behind the drug layer; nifedipine GITS works this way.`,`Ion exchange resinates release drug only when gut ions displace it, which makes release relatively independent of the tablet but dependent on the patient diet.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The release equations`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Zero order: Q = k0 t`,caption:`Q = amount released at time t, k0 = zero-order release constant in mg per hour. A plot of cumulative amount released against time is a straight line, and the rate is independent of how much drug remains.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`First order: log Qr = log Q0 - k t / 2.303`,caption:`Qr = amount remaining, Q0 = initial amount, k = first-order rate constant per hour. A plot of log amount remaining against time is linear; the rate falls as the tablet empties.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Higuchi: Q = KH x square root of t`,caption:`Q = amount released per unit area, KH = Higuchi dissolution constant. A plot of Q against the square root of time is linear. It applies to a non-swelling, non-eroding matrix under sink conditions where the initial drug loading greatly exceeds drug solubility.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Korsmeyer-Peppas: Mt / M(infinity) = K t^n`,caption:`Mt/M(infinity) = fraction released at time t, K = kinetic constant, n = release exponent. Fit only the first 60 percent of the release curve.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`n for a cylinder`,`n for a slab`,`Mechanism`],rows:[[`0.45`,`0.50`,`Fickian diffusion (Case I)`],[`0.45 to 0.89`,`0.50 to 1.00`,`Anomalous or non-Fickian, diffusion plus relaxation`],[`0.89`,`1.00`,`Case II transport, polymer relaxation controlled, zero order`],[`Above 0.89`,`Above 1.00`,`Super Case II transport`]],caption:`For a sphere the corresponding values are 0.43 and 0.85. Quote the geometry whenever you quote an n value.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Reading n`,text:`n at the low end means the drug diffuses out of a rigid matrix; n at the high end means the polymer chains relaxing and swelling control the release, which is what gives zero order; anything in between is a mixture of the two.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Gastroretentive systems`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Floating, effervescent: sodium bicarbonate with citric or tartaric acid generates carbon dioxide inside a hydrocolloid gel, lowering density below that of gastric fluid.`,`Floating, non-effervescent: a hydrodynamically balanced system in which HPMC swells into a low-density gel raft.`,`Swelling or plug type: the tablet swells beyond the pyloric diameter and cannot pass.`,`Mucoadhesive: polymers such as carbopol or chitosan stick to gastric mucus.`,`High density: densities above about 2.5 g per cubic centimetre from barium sulphate or zinc oxide let the unit settle in the antral folds.`,`All floating systems need enough gastric fluid, and they work far better in the fed state and with the patient upright.`]}]},{topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced work on controlled release is quantitative: designing the dose, checking the model, and proving similarity between profiles.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Zero-order release rate k0r = Css x CL`,caption:`k0r = required release rate in mg per hour, Css = target steady-state plasma concentration in mg per litre, CL = total body clearance in litre per hour. Maintenance dose = k0r x intended duration.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Total dose = Di + (k0r x h);  Di corrected = Di - (k0r x Tp)`,caption:`Di = loading (initial) dose, h = intended hours of release, Tp = time to peak of the immediate-release fraction. The correction avoids overshooting when the loading dose is still being absorbed as the maintenance portion begins releasing.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Worked design numerical`,text:`A drug has CL = 6 L per hour and a target Css of 10 mg per litre, and the product must release for 12 hours. Release rate k0r = 10 x 6 = 60 mg per hour, so the maintenance dose is 60 x 12 = 720 mg. If half-life is 4 h, Kel = 0.693/4 = 0.173 per hour and Vd = CL/Kel = 6/0.173 = 34.6 L, giving a loading dose of 10 x 34.6 = 346 mg. Total about 1066 mg.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Model`,`Plot that is linear`,`Suggests`],rows:[[`Zero order`,`Cumulative amount released vs t`,`Osmotic pump, reservoir device`],[`First order`,`Log amount remaining vs t`,`Porous matrix, conventional dissolution`],[`Higuchi`,`Cumulative release vs square root of t`,`Diffusion from an inert matrix`],[`Hixson-Crowell`,`Cube root of amount remaining vs t`,`Erosion with changing surface area`],[`Korsmeyer-Peppas`,`Log fraction released vs log t`,`Slope gives n and hence the mechanism`]],caption:`Choose the model with the highest correlation coefficient, then read the mechanism from the Peppas exponent.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`f2 = 50 x log { [1 + (1/n) x sum of (Rt - Tt) squared] ^ -0.5 x 100 }`,caption:`f2 = similarity factor, n = number of sampling points, Rt and Tt = percent dissolved from reference and test at each point. An f2 between 50 and 100 means the profiles are similar; the difference factor f1 should be below 15.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Level A in vitro to in vivo correlation is point-to-point between the whole dissolution curve and the whole absorption curve, and is the only level that can support a biowaiver.`,`Level B compares mean dissolution time with mean residence time using statistical moments; Level C relates a single dissolution point to a single pharmacokinetic parameter.`,`Alcohol-induced dose dumping is tested by running dissolution in media containing up to 40 percent ethanol, because some matrices dissolve in alcohol and release the whole dose.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Polymers you should be able to name`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Polymer`,`Behaviour`,`Use`],rows:[[`HPMC K4M, K15M, K100M`,`Swells into a gel layer`,`Hydrophilic matrix tablets`],[`Ethylcellulose`,`Water insoluble, permeable`,`Rate-controlling coats, matrices`],[`Eudragit RL and RS`,`Insoluble, pH independent; RL more permeable`,`Sustained release coats`],[`Eudragit L100 and S100`,`Dissolve above pH 6.0 and pH 7.0`,`Enteric and colon targeting`],[`Cellulose acetate`,`Semipermeable to water only`,`Osmotic pump membrane`],[`Carbopol, chitosan`,`Mucoadhesive, pH sensitive swelling`,`Bioadhesive and gastroretentive forms`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Zero order in vitro is not zero order in vivo`,text:`An osmotic pump keeps a constant release rate only while solid drug remains in the core to keep the internal solution saturated; the final 20 to 30 percent is released at a falling rate. Add to that a gastrointestinal transit of only about 8 to 12 hours, and a truly once-daily oral zero-order profile is a design compromise rather than a mathematical certainty.`}]},{topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A conventional injection spreads the drug through the whole body, so healthy tissue receives as much as the diseased tissue. Targeted delivery packs the drug inside a tiny carrier that concentrates it where it is needed, which raises the effect at the target and lowers toxicity everywhere else.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The carriers`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Liposome: a vesicle made of one or more phospholipid bilayers around an aqueous core. Water-soluble drug sits in the core, lipid-soluble drug in the bilayer.`,`Niosome: the same idea but the bilayer is made of non-ionic surfactants such as Span or Tween with cholesterol, so it is cheaper and does not oxidise.`,`Nanoparticle: a solid polymeric particle of roughly 10 to 1000 nm. A nanosphere is a matrix, a nanocapsule has a liquid core inside a polymer shell.`,`Solid lipid nanoparticle: a nanoparticle whose core is a lipid that is solid at body temperature, stabilised by surfactant.`,`Microsphere: a spherical matrix particle of about 1 to 1000 micrometre, usually of albumin or a biodegradable polyester.`,`Dendrimer: a synthetic, highly branched, monodisperse molecule built outward from a core in layers called generations, with cavities inside and functional groups on the surface.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Which drug goes where in a liposome`,text:`Hydrophilic drug in the aqueous compartments, lipophilic drug tucked into the lipid bilayer, amphiphilic drug at the interface. Cholesterol is added not as a drug but to stiffen the bilayer and stop leakage.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Two ways to reach a target`},{kind:`table`,headers:[``,`Passive targeting`,`Active targeting`],rows:[[`Basis`,`Natural fate of the particle: size, charge, leaky vessels`,`A ligand on the carrier binds a receptor on the target cell`],[`Example`,`Nanoparticles accumulating in a tumour by the EPR effect`,`Folate, transferrin or antibody-coated carriers`],[`Selectivity`,`Moderate`,`High, but only where the receptor is over-expressed`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`The enhanced permeability and retention effect, or EPR, is the reason passive targeting to tumours works at all. Tumour blood vessels grow fast and are leaky, so particles escape into the tumour tissue; the tumour also has poor lymphatic drainage, so once the particles are in they stay. Normal capillaries are tight and do not let the particles through.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Marketed products to name`,text:`Liposomal amphotericin B for systemic fungal infection, PEGylated liposomal doxorubicin for ovarian cancer and Kaposi sarcoma, and albumin-bound paclitaxel nanoparticles. These three appear again and again as examples of nanocarriers in clinical use.`},{kind:`para`,text:`The body defends itself against foreign particles. Plasma proteins called opsonins coat them, and macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system in liver and spleen then remove them within minutes. Coating the carrier with polyethylene glycol hides it from the opsonins and makes it long-circulating, which is why such carriers are called stealth.`}]},{topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`You need the liposome classification, the preparation methods, the size-versus-fate rule, and the structure and named examples of antibody-drug conjugates.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Liposome classification by size and lamellarity`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Abbreviation`,`Size`,`Feature`],rows:[[`Small unilamellar vesicle`,`SUV`,`20 to 100 nm`,`One bilayer, small aqueous volume`],[`Large unilamellar vesicle`,`LUV`,`Above 100 nm`,`One bilayer, high aqueous entrapment`],[`Multilamellar vesicle`,`MLV`,`0.5 to 5 micrometre`,`Onion-like, many bilayers, best for lipophilic drug`],[`Multivesicular vesicle`,`MVV`,`Above 1 micrometre`,`Vesicles within a vesicle, depot release`]]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Functional class`,`Modification`,`Purpose`],rows:[[`Conventional`,`Neutral or charged phospholipid`,`Rapidly cleared by the RES`],[`Stealth`,`PEG chains on the surface`,`Long circulation, EPR-based targeting`],[`Immunoliposome`,`Antibody or fragment attached`,`Active targeting`],[`Cationic`,`Positively charged lipid`,`Binds DNA and RNA for gene delivery`],[`pH sensitive`,`Lipid destabilised at low pH`,`Releases in the endosome`],[`Thermosensitive`,`Lipid melting near 41 degrees C`,`Release triggered by local heating`]]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Thin film hydration (Bangham method): dissolve lipid in chloroform, evaporate to a film, hydrate with buffer. Gives MLVs.`,`Size reduction: probe sonication or extrusion through polycarbonate membranes converts MLVs to SUVs.`,`Solvent injection: ether or ethanol injection of the lipid solution into warm aqueous phase.`,`Reverse phase evaporation: forms a water-in-oil emulsion first, giving large unilamellar vesicles with high entrapment.`,`Detergent removal by dialysis or gel filtration for delicate proteins.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Size decides fate`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Particle size`,`Where it goes`],rows:[[`Above 7 micrometre`,`Trapped mechanically in lung capillaries`],[`0.1 to 7 micrometre`,`Phagocytosed, liver and spleen (RES)`],[`Below 100 nm`,`Can leave leaky capillaries; long circulation if PEGylated`],[`Below about 8 nm`,`Filtered out by the kidney`]],caption:`This single table answers most passive-targeting questions. Lung targeting by size is the one people forget.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Nanoparticle polymers: PLGA and PLA (biodegradable polyesters, hydrolysed to lactic and glycolic acid), polycaprolactone, chitosan, albumin, gelatin.`,`Preparation of nanoparticles: emulsion solvent evaporation, nanoprecipitation (solvent displacement), salting out, supercritical fluid, ionic gelation for chitosan.`,`Solid lipid nanoparticles are made by high pressure homogenisation, hot or cold. Their weaknesses are low drug loading and drug expulsion when the lipid recrystallises into a more ordered polymorph, which is what nanostructured lipid carriers were designed to fix by adding a liquid lipid.`,`Microspheres are made by solvent evaporation, coacervation phase separation or spray drying; magnetic microspheres containing magnetite can be held at a site by an external magnet.`,`Dendrimers such as PAMAM and PPI carry drug in internal cavities or conjugated to surface groups. Cationic amine surfaces are haemolytic and cytotoxic, so the surface is usually PEGylated or acetylated.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Ligand-mediated targeting`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Ligand`,`Receptor or target`,`Typical use`],rows:[[`Folic acid`,`Folate receptor, over-expressed on tumours`,`Solid tumour targeting`],[`Transferrin`,`Transferrin receptor, tumours and brain endothelium`,`Tumour and brain delivery`],[`Monoclonal antibody`,`Tumour surface antigen`,`Immunoliposomes, ADCs`],[`RGD peptide`,`Integrins on new blood vessels`,`Anti-angiogenic targeting`],[`Galactose or mannose`,`Asialoglycoprotein receptor, mannose receptor`,`Hepatocytes; macrophages`],[`Aptamer`,`Selected protein target`,`Investigational targeting`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`An antibody-drug conjugate is the most precise form of active targeting: a monoclonal antibody, a linker and a very potent cytotoxic payload. The antibody binds its antigen, the conjugate is internalised, and the linker is cleaved inside the cell so that the payload is released only there. The drug-to-antibody ratio is usually two to four.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Conjugate`,`Target antigen`,`Payload`],rows:[[`Trastuzumab emtansine`,`HER2`,`DM1, a maytansinoid`],[`Brentuximab vedotin`,`CD30`,`Monomethyl auristatin E`],[`Gemtuzumab ozogamicin`,`CD33`,`Calicheamicin`],[`Trastuzumab deruxtecan`,`HER2`,`A topoisomerase I inhibitor`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Why the payload must be ultra-potent`,text:`Only a small fraction of an injected antibody dose reaches the tumour, and each antibody carries only a few drug molecules. An ordinary cytotoxic would never reach a cytotoxic intracellular concentration, so ADC payloads are agents far too toxic to give on their own, such as auristatins, maytansinoids and calicheamicin.`}]},{topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced layer is the physical characterisation of a nanocarrier and the reasons targeted systems fail in practice.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Parameter`,`Method`,`Acceptance guide`],rows:[[`Particle size`,`Dynamic light scattering`,`Below 200 nm for EPR-based delivery`],[`Polydispersity index`,`Dynamic light scattering`,`Below 0.3 indicates a narrow distribution`],[`Zeta potential`,`Electrophoretic light scattering`,`Beyond plus or minus 30 mV for charge stability`],[`Entrapment efficiency`,`Ultracentrifugation or dialysis then assay`,`Percent of drug inside the carrier`],[`Morphology`,`TEM, SEM, AFM`,`Confirms shape and lamellarity`],[`Phase transition temperature`,`Differential scanning calorimetry`,`Sets bilayer rigidity and leakage`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A liposome made of a high transition temperature lipid such as distearoylphosphatidylcholine is rigid at 37 degrees C and leaks little; egg phosphatidylcholine is fluid and leaks more. Cholesterol up to about 30 mol percent stiffens fluid bilayers and fluidises rigid ones.`,`Physical instability of liposomes shows as aggregation, fusion and drug leakage; chemical instability is hydrolysis of the ester bonds and peroxidation of unsaturated acyl chains, controlled by using saturated lipids, antioxidants and lyophilisation with a cryoprotectant such as trehalose.`,`Accelerated blood clearance: a second dose of a PEGylated carrier can be cleared much faster than the first because anti-PEG IgM is induced after the first dose. The same PEG layer that gives long circulation also hinders cellular uptake once the carrier reaches the tumour, which is called the PEG dilemma.`,`Complement activation-related pseudoallergy is an infusion reaction to liposomal and micellar products that resembles anaphylaxis but is not IgE mediated, so it can occur on the first exposure and is managed by slowing the infusion.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Toxicity that follows the carrier, not the drug`,text:`PEGylated liposomal doxorubicin has much less cardiotoxicity than free doxorubicin because it stays in the circulation and avoids the myocardium, but it causes palmar-plantar erythrodysaesthesia (hand-foot syndrome) because it accumulates in skin capillaries. Changing the carrier changes the toxicity profile as much as it changes the efficacy.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Physical targeting methods: an external magnet for magnetite-loaded carriers, local hyperthermia for thermosensitive liposomes, focused ultrasound for microbubbles, and light for photodynamic agents.`,`Cationic lipid plus DNA gives a lipoplex, cationic polymer plus DNA a polyplex. Polyethyleneimine is efficient but cytotoxic in proportion to its charge density.`,`The Ringsdorf model describes a polymer-drug conjugate as a backbone carrying a solubilising group, a drug attached by a biodegradable spacer and a homing ligand.`,`The EPR effect is far more variable in human tumours than in the rodent models it was described in, which is the main reason many passively targeted nanomedicines have failed in late clinical trials.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Nanospheres versus nanocapsules, niosomes versus liposomes`,text:`A nanosphere is a solid matrix with drug dispersed throughout; a nanocapsule is a reservoir with an oily or aqueous core inside a polymer shell. A niosome differs from a liposome only in that the bilayer is built from non-ionic surfactants rather than phospholipids, which makes it cheaper, more chemically stable and free of the oxidation problem, but it still needs cholesterol.`}]},{topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A transdermal system delivers drug through intact skin into the systemic circulation at a controlled rate. The obstacle is the stratum corneum, the outermost 10 to 20 micrometre of dead, keratin-filled cells embedded in lipid, often described as a brick and mortar wall. Almost all the resistance to absorption lies there.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Stratum corneum: dead keratinised cells in a lipid matrix; the rate-limiting barrier.`,`Viable epidermis: living cells below it, no blood vessels.`,`Dermis: contains the capillaries that take the drug away, plus follicles and sweat glands.`,`Subcutaneous fat: below the dermis, can act as a depot for lipophilic drug.`]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Intercellular route: the drug winds through the lipid between the corneocytes. This is the main route for most drugs.`,`Transcellular route: straight through the cells, alternating lipid and aqueous regions.`,`Appendageal or shunt route: down hair follicles and sweat ducts. Only about 0.1 percent of the surface, but important early on and for large or charged molecules.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Why go through the skin`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Avoids hepatic first-pass metabolism and the acid and enzymes of the gut.`,`Gives steady plasma levels for days from one application.`,`Can be removed at once if an adverse effect appears, unlike an oral or injected dose.`,`Useful in vomiting or unconscious patients and improves compliance.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The limits of the transdermal route`,text:`Only potent drugs qualify: the daily dose must be small, roughly under 10 to 20 mg, molecular weight below about 500 Da, melting point below about 200 degrees C, and log P around 1 to 3 so the drug can both enter the lipid and leave into the aqueous dermis. The drug must not irritate or sensitise the skin.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The layers of a patch`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Backing membrane: an impermeable, usually occlusive outer film that stops loss of drug and moisture.`,`Drug reservoir or matrix: the drug with its polymer, solvent or gel.`,`Rate-controlling membrane: a polymer film, often ethylene vinyl acetate copolymer or microporous polypropylene, present only in reservoir type patches.`,`Pressure-sensitive adhesive: polyisobutylene, acrylate or silicone, holding the patch to the skin.`,`Release liner: a siliconised film peeled off just before application; it is packaging, not part of the delivered system.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Not everything has to go through the skin. Mucous membranes are thinner, far more permeable and richly supplied with blood, so the buccal, sublingual, nasal, pulmonary, ocular and rectal routes are all used to get drug in quickly or to avoid the liver.`}]},{topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Learn the four patch designs, the two lists of factors affecting permeation, the classes of chemical enhancer with an example each, and the one distinguishing advantage of each mucosal route.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Patch types`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Construction`,`Release control`],rows:[[`Reservoir (membrane controlled)`,`Liquid or gel drug reservoir behind a rate-controlling membrane`,`Zero order, by the membrane`],[`Drug-in-adhesive (matrix)`,`Drug dissolved or dispersed in the adhesive itself`,`By the adhesive polymer; thinnest patch`],[`Matrix dispersion (monolithic)`,`Drug in a polymer disc with adhesive around the rim`,`Diffusion from the matrix, near Higuchi`],[`Micro-reservoir (microsealed)`,`Aqueous drug suspension dispersed as droplets in a lipophilic polymer`,`Hybrid of partition and diffusion`]],caption:`The reservoir type gives the cleanest zero-order input but is the only one that can dose-dump if the membrane is cut or damaged, which is why such patches must never be trimmed.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`J = D K delta C / h`,caption:`Fick first law applied to skin. J = steady-state flux in mg per sq cm per hour, D = diffusion coefficient in the stratum corneum, K = stratum corneum to vehicle partition coefficient, delta C = concentration difference across the barrier, h = barrier thickness. The product DK/h is the permeability coefficient.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Factors affecting skin permeation`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Biological factors`,`Effect`],rows:[[`Site of application`,`Scrotum and postauricular skin are most permeable; palm and sole least`],[`Skin hydration`,`Occlusion hydrates the stratum corneum and increases permeation`],[`Age`,`Neonatal and aged skin are more permeable than adult skin`],[`Blood flow`,`Higher flow keeps sink conditions and raises flux`],[`Skin condition`,`Damaged, burnt or diseased skin loses its barrier`],[`Skin metabolism`,`Enzymes in viable epidermis can degrade drug or activate prodrugs`]]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Physicochemical factors`,`Ideal value`],rows:[[`Molecular weight`,`Below about 500 Da`],[`Partition coefficient`,`Log P about 1 to 3`],[`Melting point`,`Below about 200 degrees C, i.e. good solubility`],[`Degree of ionisation`,`The unionised species permeates`],[`Daily dose`,`Below about 10 to 20 mg`],[`Thermodynamic activity`,`Maximum in a saturated or supersaturated vehicle`]],caption:`A drug needs both lipid and water solubility: too hydrophilic and it cannot enter the stratum corneum, too lipophilic and it will not leave it.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Chemical permeation enhancers`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Example`,`How it works`],rows:[[`Water`,`Occlusion, hydration`,`Swells the corneocytes, safest enhancer`],[`Sulphoxides`,`Dimethyl sulphoxide`,`Denatures keratin, extracts lipid; irritant`],[`Azone type`,`Laurocapram (Azone)`,`Inserts into and disorders the lipid bilayers`],[`Fatty acids`,`Oleic acid`,`Kinked chain fluidises the lipid lamellae`],[`Alcohols and glycols`,`Ethanol, propylene glycol`,`Solvent action, extracts lipid, raises solubility`],[`Terpenes`,`Menthol, limonene, eucalyptol`,`Disrupt lipid packing, generally low irritancy`],[`Surfactants`,`Sodium lauryl sulphate`,`Solubilises lipid and denatures protein; irritant`],[`Urea`,`Urea`,`Hydrating and keratolytic`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Enhancement always costs something`,text:`The best enhancers act by disrupting the very barrier that protects the body, so potency and irritancy track each other closely. The ideal enhancer is pharmacologically inert, non-irritant, non-allergenic, cosmetically acceptable, and acts reversibly so that the barrier recovers when the patch is removed.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Physical enhancement`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Iontophoresis: a small direct current, usually not more than 0.5 mA per sq cm, drives ionised drug through the skin. The anode delivers cations and the cathode anions, and electroosmotic flow carries neutral molecules. Used for lidocaine, and for pilocarpine in the sweat chloride test for cystic fibrosis.`,`Sonophoresis (phonophoresis): ultrasound, low frequency around 20 kHz being the most effective, causes cavitation in the stratum corneum lipids and opens transient channels.`,`Electroporation: brief high-voltage pulses create transient aqueous pores; suits peptides and larger molecules.`,`Microneedles: micrometre-scale needles pierce the stratum corneum without reaching pain nerves in the dermis; solid, coated, dissolving and hollow types exist.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mucosal routes, one advantage each`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Route`,`Distinctive advantage`,`Main limitation`],rows:[[`Buccal`,`Avoids first pass and the dosage form can be held in place and removed`,`Lower permeability than sublingual; saliva and eating interfere`],[`Sublingual`,`Very thin, richly vascular tissue, so the fastest onset`,`Cannot be held in place, so not for sustained delivery`],[`Nasal`,`Rapid systemic absorption, avoids first pass, possible nose to brain transport`,`Only 25 to 200 microlitre can be given; mucociliary clearance in 15 to 20 min`],[`Pulmonary`,`Huge surface area with a very thin membrane; local action at a tiny dose`,`Needs particles of 1 to 5 micrometre and correct inhaler technique`],[`Ocular`,`Direct local action on the eye with minimal systemic exposure`,`Tear drainage leaves under 5 percent bioavailability`],[`Rectal`,`Partly bypasses the liver, and works when the patient is vomiting`,`Absorption is erratic and acceptability is poor`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The rectal first-pass fact`,text:`The lower and middle haemorrhoidal veins drain into the inferior vena cava and bypass the liver, while the upper haemorrhoidal vein drains into the portal vein. A suppository placed low in the rectum therefore escapes a large part, though not all, of first-pass metabolism.`}]},{topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions test the permeation mathematics, the numbers behind aerosol and ocular delivery, and the named products.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Lag time tL = h squared / (6 D)`,caption:`tL = lag time before steady-state flux is reached, h = thickness of the stratum corneum, D = diffusion coefficient within it. This is why a patch takes several hours to reach therapeutic plasma levels and why a loading dose or an initial burst layer is sometimes built in.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Required flux J = (Css x CL) / A`,caption:`Css = target steady-state plasma concentration, CL = clearance, A = patch surface area. Rearranged, it gives the patch area needed: a drug with high clearance needs an impractically large patch, which is why only potent, low-clearance drugs succeed transdermally.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Aerodynamic diameter`,`Where it deposits`,`Mechanism`],rows:[[`Above 10 micrometre`,`Oropharynx, swallowed`,`Inertial impaction`],[`5 to 10 micrometre`,`Large conducting airways`,`Impaction`],[`1 to 5 micrometre`,`Bronchioles and alveoli, the useful range`,`Sedimentation`],[`Below 0.5 micrometre`,`Largely exhaled again`,`Diffusion, too little settling time`]],caption:`The mass median aerodynamic diameter and the fine particle fraction are the two quality attributes reported for any inhaler.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Alveolar surface area is about 70 to 100 square metres with a membrane only 0.1 to 0.5 micrometre thick, which is why inhaled proteins can reach the blood at all.`,`The eye holds only 7 to 10 microlitre in the cul-de-sac while a drop delivers about 50 microlitre, and tears turn over at about 1 microlitre per minute; most of an eye drop is therefore drained within a couple of minutes.`,`Ocular contact time is extended with viscosity enhancers, gels, ointments, in-situ gelling systems and inserts such as the pilocarpine ocular insert.`,`Buccal absorption is described by a pH-partition relationship, so weak acids are absorbed better at low buccal pH and weak bases at high pH; this is the basis of the classic buccal absorption test.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Transdermal product`,`Indication`],rows:[[`Scopolamine`,`Motion sickness; the first transdermal system marketed`],[`Nitroglycerin`,`Angina prophylaxis; needs a nitrate-free interval`],[`Nicotine`,`Smoking cessation`],[`Fentanyl and buprenorphine`,`Chronic pain`],[`Oestradiol and testosterone`,`Hormone replacement`],[`Rivastigmine, rotigotine`,`Alzheimer disease, Parkinson disease`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Why tolerance forces a patch-free interval`,text:`Continuous nitroglycerin delivery produces nitrate tolerance within about 24 hours, so the patch is worn for 12 to 14 hours and removed for the rest of the day. A steady plasma level is an advantage only when the receptor does not down-regulate; for nitrates and for some agonists, fluctuation is therapeutically necessary.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Distinctions that are asked`,text:`Iontophoresis uses a small continuous current and moves ions; electroporation uses brief high-voltage pulses and makes pores; sonophoresis uses ultrasonic cavitation. Insulin cannot be given transdermally because its molecular weight of about 5800 Da is many times the 500 Da practical limit, which is why the inhaled and injected routes are used instead.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-nd-controlled-release-1`,topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`An enteric coated aspirin tablet is an example of which type of modified release?`,options:[`Sustained release`,`Controlled release`,`Delayed release`,`Repeat action`],answer:2,explanation:`A delayed release form releases the drug at a time or site other than immediately after administration, which is exactly what an enteric coat does by holding the dose until the tablet leaves the stomach. It does not slow the release once it starts, so it is not sustained or controlled. Repeat action would mean a second full dose released after an interval.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-nd-controlled-release-2`,topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The biological half-life range that makes a drug a good candidate for an oral controlled release product is`,options:[`Less than 1 hour`,`2 to 8 hours`,`10 to 15 hours`,`More than 24 hours`],answer:1,explanation:`A half-life of 2 to 8 hours is short enough that frequent dosing is a genuine problem yet long enough that the controlled release dose stays a reasonable size. Below about 2 hours the maintenance dose becomes impractically large, and above about 8 hours the drug is already effectively long acting, so formulating it adds nothing.`},{id:`q-nd-controlled-release-3`,topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Drug release from an inert non-swelling matrix under sink conditions is proportional to`,options:[`Time`,`Logarithm of time`,`Cube root of time`,`Square root of time`],answer:3,explanation:`This is the Higuchi relationship, Q = KH multiplied by the square root of t, and it arises because the diffusion path lengthens as the depletion zone moves inward. Release proportional to time itself would be zero order, which a simple matrix cannot give. The cube root relationship is Hixson-Crowell and applies to erosion with a shrinking surface area.`},{id:`q-nd-controlled-release-4`,topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In an elementary osmotic pump tablet, the cellulose acetate coat functions as`,options:[`A semipermeable membrane allowing only water to enter`,`An enteric coat dissolving above pH 6`,`A swellable hydrogel that pushes the drug out`,`An adhesive that retains the tablet in the stomach`],answer:0,explanation:`Cellulose acetate is permeable to water but not to the dissolved drug or osmogen, so water is drawn in osmotically and the saturated solution is forced out through a laser-drilled orifice at a constant rate. That is what makes the release zero order and independent of gastrointestinal pH and agitation. The swelling push layer belongs to the push-pull osmotic pump, not the elementary one.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-nd-controlled-release-5`,topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the Korsmeyer-Peppas model, a release exponent n of 0.89 for a cylindrical matrix indicates`,options:[`Fickian diffusion`,`Anomalous (non-Fickian) transport`,`Case II transport, controlled by polymer relaxation`,`Super Case II transport`],answer:2,explanation:`For a cylinder, n = 0.45 is Fickian diffusion, values between 0.45 and 0.89 are anomalous, n = 0.89 is Case II transport where polymer chain relaxation controls release and the profile is effectively zero order, and above 0.89 is super Case II. The corresponding boundaries for a slab are 0.5 and 1.0, so the geometry must always be stated with the n value.`},{id:`q-nd-controlled-release-6`,topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A drug has a clearance of 5 L per hour and a target steady-state plasma concentration of 8 mg per litre. The maintenance portion of a 12-hour zero-order product should contain`,options:[`40 mg`,`96 mg`,`480 mg`,`600 mg`],answer:2,explanation:`The required zero-order release rate is k0r = Css x CL = 8 mg per litre x 5 L per hour = 40 mg per hour. Over 12 hours the maintenance dose is 40 x 12 = 480 mg. Choosing 40 mg gives the hourly rate rather than the dose, and the loading dose is a separate calculation, Css x Vd.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-nd-targeted-carriers-1`,topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Liposomes are vesicles composed mainly of`,options:[`Non-ionic surfactants`,`Phospholipid bilayers, usually with cholesterol`,`Biodegradable polyesters such as PLGA`,`Branched polyamidoamine molecules`],answer:1,explanation:`A liposome is one or more phospholipid bilayers enclosing an aqueous core, with cholesterol added to stiffen the membrane and limit leakage. Vesicles made from non-ionic surfactants are niosomes, PLGA gives polymeric nanoparticles and polyamidoamine is a dendrimer. Hydrophilic drug sits in the aqueous core and lipophilic drug in the bilayer.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-nd-targeted-carriers-2`,topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The main advantage of a niosome over a conventional liposome is that it`,options:[`Carries only lipophilic drugs`,`Needs no cholesterol`,`Is always smaller than 20 nm`,`Is cheaper and chemically more stable, with no lipid oxidation`],answer:3,explanation:`Niosomes are built from non-ionic surfactants such as spans and tweens, which are inexpensive and are not subject to the hydrolysis and peroxidation that limit phospholipid shelf life. They still contain cholesterol to stabilise the bilayer, and like liposomes they can carry both hydrophilic and lipophilic drug. Size depends on the preparation method, not on the surfactant.`},{id:`q-nd-targeted-carriers-3`,topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The enhanced permeability and retention effect allows nanocarriers to accumulate in tumours because tumour tissue has`,options:[`Tight capillary junctions and active lymphatic drainage`,`Leaky, fenestrated vessels together with poor lymphatic drainage`,`A very low interstitial fluid pressure`,`A specific receptor for polyethylene glycol`],answer:1,explanation:`Rapid angiogenesis leaves tumour vessels with wide interendothelial gaps that nanoparticles can cross, and because lymphatic drainage is defective the particles are not carried away again, so they accumulate. Normal capillaries are tight, which gives the selectivity. EPR is the basis of passive targeting and involves no ligand or receptor at all.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-nd-targeted-carriers-4`,topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Intravenously injected particles larger than about 7 micrometre are localised mainly in the`,options:[`Lung capillaries`,`Liver and spleen`,`Kidney glomeruli`,`Bone marrow`],answer:0,explanation:`Particles above roughly 7 micrometre are mechanically trapped in the first capillary bed they meet, which for an intravenous injection is the pulmonary bed, and this is exploited for passive lung targeting. Particles of about 0.1 to 7 micrometre escape that filter and are phagocytosed by the reticuloendothelial cells of liver and spleen. Only very small particles, below about 8 nm, are filtered by the kidney.`},{id:`q-nd-targeted-carriers-5`,topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Coating a liposome with polyethylene glycol prolongs its circulation because PEG`,options:[`Increases the drug loading of the vesicle`,`Adds a positive surface charge that repels macrophages`,`Forms a hydrated layer that hinders opsonin adsorption`,`Makes the bilayer rigid and impermeable`],answer:2,explanation:`The flexible hydrated PEG chains sterically prevent plasma opsonins from binding the vesicle surface, so macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system do not recognise it and clearance is slowed; such carriers are called stealth or long-circulating. PEG is uncharged, and rigidity comes from cholesterol and the choice of lipid, not from PEG. The same PEG layer also hinders uptake by the target cell, which is the PEG dilemma.`},{id:`q-nd-targeted-carriers-6`,topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which statement about antibody-drug conjugates is correct?`,options:[`Trastuzumab emtansine targets CD30 and carries calicheamicin`,`The payload is usually a conventional cytotoxic of ordinary potency`,`A drug-to-antibody ratio above 20 is preferred for efficacy`,`Brentuximab vedotin targets CD30 and carries monomethyl auristatin E`],answer:3,explanation:`Brentuximab vedotin is an anti-CD30 antibody linked to monomethyl auristatin E; trastuzumab emtansine targets HER2 and carries the maytansinoid DM1, while calicheamicin is the payload of gemtuzumab ozogamicin. Payloads must be ultra-potent because only a small fraction of the injected antibody reaches the tumour, and the drug-to-antibody ratio is kept around two to four since higher loading causes aggregation and faster clearance.`},{id:`q-nd-transdermal-mucosal-1`,topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The rate-limiting barrier to percutaneous absorption is the`,options:[`Stratum corneum`,`Viable epidermis`,`Dermis`,`Subcutaneous fat`],answer:0,explanation:`The stratum corneum is 10 to 20 micrometre of dead keratinised cells in a lipid matrix and provides almost all of the resistance to drug entry. The viable epidermis and dermis are hydrated and freely permeable, and the dermal capillaries actually remove the drug and maintain the concentration gradient. Every chemical and physical enhancer works by acting on the stratum corneum.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-nd-transdermal-mucosal-2`,topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which component of a transdermal patch is discarded before the patch is applied?`,options:[`Backing membrane`,`Rate-controlling membrane`,`Release liner`,`Pressure-sensitive adhesive`],answer:2,explanation:`The release liner is a siliconised film that protects the adhesive during storage and is peeled off immediately before use, so it is regarded as part of the packaging rather than the delivery system. The backing membrane stays on the outside as an occlusive barrier, and the rate-controlling membrane and adhesive both remain in contact with the skin.`},{id:`q-nd-transdermal-mucosal-3`,topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`For a drug to be a realistic transdermal candidate, its molecular weight and log P should be about`,options:[`Below 500 Da and log P 1 to 3`,`Below 500 Da and log P above 6`,`Above 2000 Da and log P 1 to 3`,`Any molecular weight provided the drug is water soluble`],answer:0,explanation:`Diffusion through the lipid of the stratum corneum falls off sharply above about 500 Da, and a log P of roughly 1 to 3 balances the need to partition into the lipid barrier with the need to partition out again into the aqueous dermis. A very lipophilic drug with a log P above 6 enters the stratum corneum easily but then remains stuck there. This size limit is why insulin, at about 5800 Da, cannot be delivered through intact skin.`},{id:`q-nd-transdermal-mucosal-4`,topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Sublingual administration of glyceryl trinitrate gives a rapid effect chiefly because the sublingual mucosa`,options:[`Has a thick keratinised epithelium that stores the drug`,`Contains enzymes that activate the drug`,`Has a very low pH that favours absorption`,`Is thin and richly vascular, and drainage bypasses the liver`],answer:3,explanation:`The sublingual epithelium is thin and non-keratinised over a dense capillary bed draining into the systemic circulation, so absorption is fast and hepatic first-pass metabolism is avoided, which matters greatly for a drug as extensively metabolised as glyceryl trinitrate. The buccal mucosa is less permeable but can hold a dosage form in place, which is why it is the route chosen for sustained mucosal delivery rather than the sublingual route.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-nd-transdermal-mucosal-5`,topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The aerodynamic particle size range required for deposition in the bronchioles and alveoli is`,options:[`0.1 to 0.5 micrometre`,`1 to 5 micrometre`,`5 to 10 micrometre`,`10 to 20 micrometre`],answer:1,explanation:`Particles of 1 to 5 micrometre are small enough to escape inertial impaction in the oropharynx yet large enough to sediment in the small airways, so this is the respirable fraction. Particles above 10 micrometre impact in the throat and are swallowed, while particles below about 0.5 micrometre stay suspended and are largely exhaled again.`},{id:`q-nd-transdermal-mucosal-6`,topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Taking the stratum corneum as 20 micrometre thick with a drug diffusion coefficient of 1 x 10^-9 sq cm per second, the lag time before steady-state flux is about`,options:[`11 seconds`,`11 minutes`,`1.9 hours`,`11 hours`],answer:1,explanation:`Lag time tL = h squared/(6D). Here h = 20 micrometre = 2 x 10^-3 cm, so h squared = 4 x 10^-6 sq cm, and tL = 4 x 10^-6 / (6 x 10^-9) = 667 seconds, which is about 11 minutes. The commonest slip is leaving h in micrometre while D is in square centimetre units, which changes the answer by many orders of magnitude.`,tag:`Numerical`}],n=[{id:`f-nd-controlled-release-1`,topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,front:`Sustained versus controlled release?`,back:`Sustained: prolongs duration, rate usually first order and falling. Controlled: predetermined, usually zero-order rate holding plasma level steady.`},{id:`f-nd-controlled-release-2`,topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,front:`Ideal candidate properties for oral controlled release?`,back:`Half-life 2 to 8 h, dose under about 1 g, wide therapeutic index, MW under 1000 Da, log P 1 to 3, absorbed all along the gut.`},{id:`f-nd-controlled-release-3`,topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,front:`Higuchi equation?`,back:`Q = KH x square root of t. Diffusion from an inert, non-swelling matrix under sink conditions.`},{id:`f-nd-controlled-release-4`,topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,front:`Korsmeyer-Peppas n values for a cylinder?`,back:`0.45 Fickian; 0.45 to 0.89 anomalous; 0.89 Case II (zero order); above 0.89 super Case II. For a slab, 0.5 and 1.0.`},{id:`f-nd-controlled-release-5`,topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,front:`Which mechanisms give true zero-order release?`,back:`Reservoir (membrane-controlled) diffusion systems and osmotic pumps.`},{id:`f-nd-controlled-release-6`,topicId:`nd-controlled-release`,front:`Zero-order release rate needed for a target Css?`,back:`k0r = Css x CL; maintenance dose = k0r x hours of release; loading dose = Css x Vd.`},{id:`f-nd-targeted-carriers-1`,topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,front:`Liposome types by lamellarity and size?`,back:`SUV 20 to 100 nm, LUV above 100 nm, MLV 0.5 to 5 micrometre, MVV above 1 micrometre.`},{id:`f-nd-targeted-carriers-2`,topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,front:`What is the EPR effect?`,back:`Enhanced permeability and retention: leaky tumour vessels let nanocarriers in and poor lymphatic drainage keeps them there. Basis of passive targeting.`},{id:`f-nd-targeted-carriers-3`,topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,front:`Fate of intravenous particles by size?`,back:`Above 7 micrometre lung capillaries; 0.1 to 7 micrometre liver and spleen (RES); below 100 nm long circulating if PEGylated; below 8 nm renal.`},{id:`f-nd-targeted-carriers-4`,topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,front:`Passive versus active targeting?`,back:`Passive relies on particle size, charge and the EPR effect. Active uses a ligand such as folate, transferrin, RGD peptide or an antibody.`},{id:`f-nd-targeted-carriers-5`,topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,front:`Three parts of an antibody-drug conjugate?`,back:`Monoclonal antibody, cleavable or non-cleavable linker, ultra-potent cytotoxic payload; drug-to-antibody ratio usually 2 to 4.`},{id:`f-nd-targeted-carriers-6`,topicId:`nd-targeted-carriers`,front:`SLN weakness and its fix?`,back:`Low drug loading and expulsion when the lipid recrystallises; solved by adding a liquid lipid to give a nanostructured lipid carrier.`},{id:`f-nd-transdermal-mucosal-1`,topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,front:`Layers of a transdermal patch, outside inward?`,back:`Backing membrane, drug reservoir or matrix, rate-controlling membrane, pressure-sensitive adhesive, release liner (peeled off).`},{id:`f-nd-transdermal-mucosal-2`,topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,front:`Four transdermal patch designs?`,back:`Reservoir (membrane controlled), drug-in-adhesive matrix, matrix dispersion (monolithic), micro-reservoir (microsealed).`},{id:`f-nd-transdermal-mucosal-3`,topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,front:`Ideal properties for a transdermal drug?`,back:`MW under 500 Da, log P 1 to 3, melting point under 200 degrees C, daily dose under 10 to 20 mg, non-irritant.`},{id:`f-nd-transdermal-mucosal-4`,topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,front:`Name one enhancer from each major class.`,back:`Sulphoxide: DMSO. Azone type: laurocapram. Fatty acid: oleic acid. Glycol: propylene glycol. Terpene: menthol. Surfactant: sodium lauryl sulphate.`},{id:`f-nd-transdermal-mucosal-5`,topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,front:`Iontophoresis versus sonophoresis versus electroporation?`,back:`Iontophoresis: small direct current moves ions (anode delivers cations). Sonophoresis: ultrasonic cavitation. Electroporation: brief high-voltage pulses making transient pores.`},{id:`f-nd-transdermal-mucosal-6`,topicId:`nd-transdermal-mucosal`,front:`One advantage each: sublingual, nasal, pulmonary, ocular, rectal.`,back:`Sublingual fastest onset; nasal avoids first pass with possible nose-to-brain access; pulmonary huge thin surface and tiny local dose; ocular local action with little systemic exposure; rectal partly bypasses the liver and works in vomiting.`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};